# What a creature IS, on top of how it walks.
#
# Core's behaviors are about locomotion and deliberately nothing else. A record says a body holds its
# tile, ambles, closes on what it notices, opens the gap, or tidies litter — five ways of moving, none
# of which mentions fighting. The original's five said why instead, and four of them meant something
# about combat.
#
# This file is the translation. Nothing here is new engine surface; it is the original's vocabulary
# written out of the parts Core does offer.
#
#   Attack on sight        Pursue, and swing on contact                       — Rules.OnContact
#   Attack when attacked   Wander, and chase whoever lands a hit              — Beasts.Answer
#   Friendly               Wander, with no rule pointing it at anybody
#   Stationary             Stationary
#   Guard                  Wander, carrying the guard key, roused by a crime  — Beasts.CallGuards
#
# ⚠ A guard is marked on the CREATURE RECORD, in the attributes an editor hangs on the template. That
# is where a per-species fact belongs: every copy of that creature is a guard, and a body that became
# one at spawn would be a guard by accident.

shared model Beasts

    # Carried by a creature that answers a crime. Any number above zero means yes.
    public string Guard = "guard";

    # How far a shout carries, in tiles. The original asked each guard for its own noticing range;
    # one number is the same rule with the authoring taken out of it.
    public integer GuardRange = 10;

    # How many answer. A whole map converging on one body reads as a bug rather than as law.
    public integer GuardsWhoAnswer = 3;

    # ── Fighting back ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    # Every creature answers a hit, whatever its record says about noticing.
    #
    # For one authored to close on what it sees this changes nothing; it was already coming. For one
    # authored to amble this is "attacks when attacked" entire — it never notices anybody, and nothing
    # else ever points it at somebody. For one authored to open the gap it runs, as a body that flees
    # should when it is hit.
    public function Answer(Npc it, Player who)
        # Whether it turned is dropped on purpose. A handle whose body has already left cannot be
        # pointed anywhere, and the creature that was hit being gone is the outcome the hitter wanted.
        it.Chase(who);
    end function

    # And the same between two creatures, so a bite from behind turns the bitten one around rather
    # than leaving it walking away from a fight it is losing.
    public function AnswerNpc(Npc it, Npc attacker)
        it.ChaseNpc(attacker);
    end function

    # ── Guards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    # Somebody did something the town will not have. The guards standing near it come after them.
    #
    # ⚠ World.NpcsNear answers nearest first, so taking the first few takes the ones who could
    # plausibly have seen it.
    public function CallGuards(Player on, integer map, integer x, integer y)
        Npc[] nearby = World.NpcsNear(map, x, y, Beasts.GuardRange);
        integer sent = 0;

        loop each body in nearby
            if sent >= Beasts.GuardsWhoAnswer
                yield;
            end if

            if body.Number(Beasts.Guard) > 0
                if body.Chase(on)
                    sent = sent + 1;
                end if
            end if
        end loop

        if sent > 0
            on.Message("The guard has seen that.");
        end if
    end function

end model
